1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:09,000
Incredible power, deceiving simplicity, top gun and top secret.

2
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:13,540
We trust our lives to them.

3
00:00:15,460 --> 00:00:19,640
Now, Jet Engines on Modern Marvels.

4
00:00:34,243 --> 00:00:39,672
The SR-71 Blackbird. In the opinion of many, the greatest airplane ever made.

5
00:00:39,673 --> 00:00:44,255
Its two huge jet engines have the power of 45 diesel locomotives.

6
00:00:44,256 --> 00:00:50,460
It can fly from Los Angeles to New York in under an hour at speeds faster than a bullet.

7
00:00:50,461 --> 00:00:57,300
The Blackbird, designed in the early 60s, is the crowning glory of one of the greatest

8
00:00:57,301 --> 00:01:06,320
eras of technological change in the history of mankind.

9
00:01:06,321 --> 00:01:11,420
The Jet Age, the term was synonymous with scientific and social progress in the 50s

10
00:01:11,421 --> 00:01:12,860
and early 60s.

11
00:01:12,861 --> 00:01:17,920
The Jet Age conjured up not just a technological revolution, but a radical change in the way

12
00:01:17,921 --> 00:01:24,920
human beings thought, acted and lived and the jet age was powered by this.

13
00:01:24,921 --> 00:01:30,680
The blast of hot gases out of the end of a metal tube, one of the simplest ideas for

14
00:01:30,681 --> 00:01:34,100
an engine in history.

15
00:01:34,101 --> 00:01:38,780
Any child who's ever blown up a balloon then released it to zoom through the air as it

16
00:01:38,781 --> 00:01:43,480
deflates has seen how jet power works.

17
00:01:43,481 --> 00:01:46,600
The most common form of jet engine today is the turbojet.

18
00:01:46,601 --> 00:01:52,180
It uses spinning compressor wheels at the front end of the engine to compress the incoming air.

19
00:01:52,980 --> 00:01:55,400
The air is then mixed with fuel and ignited.

20
00:01:56,060 --> 00:01:59,620
The blast of exhaust gases out the back propels the engine forward,

21
00:02:00,160 --> 00:02:06,080
as well as rotating turbine wheels at the back, which turn the front compressor wheels on the same shaft.

22
00:02:07,580 --> 00:02:12,400
Today's jet engine was a logical progression from a device called a turbo supercharger

23
00:02:12,401 --> 00:02:15,520
that was first used in the waning years of World War I.

24
00:02:15,521 --> 00:02:20,200
to get more power out of military airplanes powered by piston engines.

25
00:02:21,360 --> 00:02:26,600
Here was a case where technologists were trying to allow the piston engine to breathe, if you will,

26
00:02:26,900 --> 00:02:28,360
more efficiently at high altitude.

27
00:02:28,980 --> 00:02:34,760
With a turbine and the exhaust that rotated a compressor wheel to force-feed air into the engine,

28
00:02:35,380 --> 00:02:39,880
they had the main elements of a jet engine, although they didn't realize it at the time.

29
00:02:40,840 --> 00:02:44,460
Ironically, for all their insight in developing these turbines,

30
00:02:44,461 --> 00:02:48,300
which had to operate in a very high-temperature exhaust environment.

31
00:02:48,540 --> 00:02:51,480
For all that insight, they missed the significance

32
00:02:51,481 --> 00:02:54,780
of directly connecting the turbine to the compressor.

33
00:02:55,620 --> 00:02:59,860
Propeller-driven piston-powered aircraft had a built-in speed limitation

34
00:02:59,861 --> 00:03:04,340
because propeller blades lose sound.

35
00:03:05,340 --> 00:03:10,240
In the early 1930s, a few far-sighted aircraft engineers

36
00:03:10,241 --> 00:03:12,500
in Britain, Germany, France, and Italy

37
00:03:12,501 --> 00:03:18,520
began toying with the idea of eliminating the piston engine and the propeller altogether.

38
00:03:20,080 --> 00:03:23,820
It might be possible, they thought, to power a plane with a device

39
00:03:23,821 --> 00:03:29,260
not all that different from a turbo supercharger, but bigger and more powerful.

40
00:03:30,640 --> 00:03:35,300
Critics, however, warned it was folly to try to use a pure jet engine to power an airplane.

41
00:03:35,980 --> 00:03:39,160
The scoffers said such a jet engine would have to be so big and heavy

42
00:03:39,161 --> 00:03:40,980
the plane could never get off the ground.

43
00:03:40,981 --> 00:03:47,980
Some claimed the jet engine would have to be lined with bricks to protect the fuselage and the pilot from the intense heat.

44
00:03:47,981 --> 00:03:50,980
But a few young scientists persisted.

45
00:03:50,981 --> 00:03:57,980
One of them was Britain's Frank Whittle, who first wrote about the idea of jet aircraft in 1928,

46
00:03:57,981 --> 00:04:06,980
when as a cadet at Cranwell Military School he turned in a thesis on the possibilities of powering airplanes with a gas turbine engine or turbojet.

47
00:04:06,981 --> 00:04:08,800
or turbojet.

48
00:04:08,801 --> 00:04:11,980
After graduation from Cambridge University,

49
00:04:11,981 --> 00:04:15,580
Whittle began putting together a demonstration turbojet.

50
00:04:15,581 --> 00:04:19,140
His first model ran so erratically it almost exploded,

51
00:04:19,141 --> 00:04:22,620
and it would be three and a half years before he had a working model.

52
00:04:22,621 --> 00:04:25,720
But it was too weak to power an airplane.

53
00:04:25,721 --> 00:04:29,420
Whittle had been given unusual treatment by the Royal Air Force.

54
00:04:29,421 --> 00:04:32,960
He was placed in selected positions so that he could continue to develop his

55
00:04:32,961 --> 00:04:35,040
radical ideas.

56
00:04:35,041 --> 00:04:39,580
The irony of it is that when it came time to really back his ideas,

57
00:04:39,760 --> 00:04:41,140
the British government backed away from it.

58
00:04:41,400 --> 00:04:43,300
He didn't get the support that he needed.

59
00:04:43,960 --> 00:04:47,140
Whittle had no way of knowing that at the same time in Germany,

60
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:51,340
another bright young engineer was working on exactly the same idea.

61
00:04:52,220 --> 00:04:56,960
Hans von Ohain had convinced one of Germany's leading airplane manufacturers,

62
00:04:56,961 --> 00:05:02,120
Ernst Heinkel, to let him build an experimental turbojet in 1936.

63
00:05:02,600 --> 00:05:04,840
Within a year, he had a viable engine.

64
00:05:05,040 --> 00:05:13,000
And in August of 1939, the Heinkel He 178 rolled down the runway and lifted into the sky.

65
00:05:13,780 --> 00:05:16,280
The world was about to change.

66
00:05:17,160 --> 00:05:19,440
The first jet engine was in the air.

67
00:05:23,460 --> 00:05:29,180
Germany was not about to announce to the world that it had just created the first turbojet airplane.

68
00:05:29,880 --> 00:05:33,860
The German Air Ministry recognized that they had a potential wonder weapon,

69
00:05:33,861 --> 00:05:37,780
and they also realized they would be at war very soon.

70
00:05:40,020 --> 00:05:44,480
The ratio of the speed of an airplane to the speed of sound called the Mach number

71
00:05:44,481 --> 00:05:49,320
was determined by the Austrian scientist Ernst Mach in 1887.

72
00:05:50,120 --> 00:05:53,700
Jet engines will return on Modern Marvels.

73
00:05:55,560 --> 00:06:00,200
We now return to Jet Engines on Modern Marvels.

74
00:06:03,860 --> 00:06:09,480
In September of 1939, less than a month after the test flight of the world's first jet fighter,

75
00:06:09,960 --> 00:06:11,200
Germany invaded Poland.

76
00:06:11,840 --> 00:06:12,860
The war was on.

77
00:06:13,640 --> 00:06:17,620
But the work on getting a jet fighter into mass production still proceeded slowly.

78
00:06:18,880 --> 00:06:23,580
In 1939 and 40, there was no great impetus in Germany to develop the jet engine.

79
00:06:24,260 --> 00:06:29,100
The Luftwaffe felt invincible, and their propeller-driven aircraft were doing just fine.

80
00:06:29,101 --> 00:06:35,000
In early 1940, they destroy the Low Countries and the Scandinavian countries,

81
00:06:35,540 --> 00:06:36,900
and then they're threatening Great Britain.

82
00:06:37,060 --> 00:06:37,940
And what happens?

83
00:06:38,480 --> 00:06:41,500
In the late summer and the fall of 1940,

84
00:06:42,120 --> 00:06:44,360
Germany loses the Battle of Britain.

85
00:06:45,220 --> 00:06:48,540
Now, that is the moment when you start seeing a stimulus.

86
00:06:49,180 --> 00:06:51,560
Now they suddenly realize we need something else.

87
00:06:52,180 --> 00:06:55,660
Although Ernst Heinkel had flown the first jet-powered test plane,

88
00:06:55,661 --> 00:07:03,420
He was now in a race with his arch rival, Willie Messerschmidt, to develop the first mass production jet fighter for the Luftwaffe.

89
00:07:04,180 --> 00:07:11,900
In April of 1941, Heinkel was the first into the air with his fighter prototype, the innovative HE-280.

90
00:07:12,480 --> 00:07:18,600
But a year later, his competitor Messerschmidt had an even better jet fighter, the ME-262.

91
00:07:19,520 --> 00:07:26,880
The 262 was a superior airplane, had higher speed, higher range, better arm package, so it was selected for production.

92
00:07:27,080 --> 00:07:30,240
It was designed basically to destroy American bombers.

93
00:07:30,440 --> 00:07:34,300
It could do that very well because it had a tremendous speed advantage over them.

94
00:07:34,600 --> 00:07:37,360
It had a tremendously powerful cannon armament to do that.

95
00:07:38,220 --> 00:07:42,140
17 ME-262s were ordered for tests and evaluation.

96
00:07:42,141 --> 00:07:47,420
When Nazi air ace Adolf Gallen came to Messerschmitt's test center to fly one,

97
00:07:47,840 --> 00:07:50,800
he was amazed at its effortless power and speed,

98
00:07:51,220 --> 00:07:55,120
which far surpassed the performance of the Luftwaffe's piston engine planes.

99
00:07:55,800 --> 00:07:59,440
Accompanied by only a whistling sound, my jet shot through the air.

100
00:08:00,040 --> 00:08:02,300
This is not a step forward, this is a leap.

101
00:08:02,860 --> 00:08:05,440
It will guarantee us an unbelievable advantage,

102
00:08:05,740 --> 00:08:08,340
so long as the enemy sticks to piston propulsion.

103
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:14,260
Meanwhile, in England, Rolls-Royce had taken over development of Frank Whittle's turbojet,

104
00:08:14,360 --> 00:08:19,680
and together with the Gloucester Aircraft Company, they created the Gloucester Meteor twin-engine fighter,

105
00:08:20,160 --> 00:08:23,800
which had its first test flight in March of 1943.

106
00:08:26,620 --> 00:08:30,760
But the Germans were the first to send squadrons of jet fighters into battle.

107
00:08:32,180 --> 00:08:36,540
225 Me 262s were delivered to the Luftwaffe in 1944.

108
00:08:36,541 --> 00:08:42,740
Allied pilots would soon be shocked to see this completely new bird of prey swooping down on them

109
00:08:42,741 --> 00:08:45,500
at speeds that seemed impossibly fast.

110
00:08:46,140 --> 00:08:49,100
I think from an Allied pilot's standpoint, what they were astonished at

111
00:08:49,101 --> 00:08:54,200
was encountering a fighter that could put its nose up and seemingly defy gravity

112
00:08:54,201 --> 00:08:56,660
and simply climb vertically away from them.

113
00:08:57,080 --> 00:08:58,240
That came as a real shock.

114
00:08:58,940 --> 00:09:03,880
U.S. fighter pilot Bob Hoover, shot down over the Mediterranean in 1944,

115
00:09:03,881 --> 00:09:07,440
saw his first jet from a German prison camp.

116
00:09:08,160 --> 00:09:10,980
I thought, my goodness, we've lost the war

117
00:09:10,981 --> 00:09:12,960
because it was going so fast.

118
00:09:13,840 --> 00:09:17,460
I couldn't believe that there was anything like that in existence.

119
00:09:17,461 --> 00:09:20,900
And I thought, boy, I might be in this place forever.

120
00:09:21,700 --> 00:09:25,820
In one of the largest aerial armadas in history in March of 1945,

121
00:09:26,600 --> 00:09:30,900
the Allies sent 1,200 flying fortress bombers to attack Berlin,

122
00:09:30,901 --> 00:09:34,740
escorted by more than 600 piston engine fighters.

123
00:09:35,680 --> 00:09:40,860
The Germans scrambled 37 Me 262 jet fighters to intercept them

124
00:09:40,861 --> 00:09:44,800
and the lightning fast jets, although outnumbered nearly 50 to 1,

125
00:09:45,360 --> 00:09:49,020
managed to down eight Allied bombers and one fighter.

126
00:09:50,480 --> 00:09:55,260
But the Me 262 came too late to make a difference in the war's outcome.

127
00:09:55,940 --> 00:09:59,760
From 1944 onwards, as the war worsened for the Nazis,

128
00:09:59,761 --> 00:10:03,640
German jet design began to show signs of desperation.

129
00:10:04,800 --> 00:10:07,800
Some of these fighter concepts were utterly bizarre.

130
00:10:08,400 --> 00:10:12,800
And so you started seeing weird little fighters like the Heinkel 162,

131
00:10:12,920 --> 00:10:15,420
the so-called Volksjäger, built out of plywood

132
00:10:15,421 --> 00:10:17,940
with a jet engine on the back of the airplane behind the pilot,

133
00:10:18,320 --> 00:10:20,220
or the Messerschmitt 163 Comet,

134
00:10:20,640 --> 00:10:24,180
which was a rocket-propelled fighter that used two hypergolic fuels.

135
00:10:24,280 --> 00:10:27,080
In other words, when the fuels were mixed, they would spontaneously combust.

136
00:10:27,081 --> 00:10:30,740
that was actually far more dangerous to its pilots than it ever was to the Allies.

137
00:10:32,260 --> 00:10:36,260
You had piloted surface-to-air missiles, the Bauckham Natter.

138
00:10:36,261 --> 00:10:40,760
You had piloted air-to-surface guided bombs.

139
00:10:41,600 --> 00:10:43,900
Many of these would have required years.

140
00:10:44,460 --> 00:10:47,040
Some of their projects would have required decades to develop.

141
00:10:47,480 --> 00:10:53,560
The price of these ineffective projects was fewer effective planes like the Me-262

142
00:10:53,561 --> 00:10:58,300
and the Arado 234, the world's first jet bomber.

143
00:10:59,660 --> 00:11:02,040
Meanwhile, the British, led by Frank Whittle,

144
00:11:02,400 --> 00:11:05,880
had their first jet fighter ready to go in late 1944.

145
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:08,860
Called the Gloucester Meteor,

146
00:11:09,180 --> 00:11:11,860
it helped intercept German V-1 buzz bombs,

147
00:11:12,520 --> 00:11:15,420
which were powered by a pulse jet,

148
00:11:15,700 --> 00:11:18,140
a jet engine that took intermittent gulfs of air,

149
00:11:18,560 --> 00:11:21,400
producing the strange sound that gave the buzz bomb its name.

150
00:11:21,401 --> 00:11:28,400
The British Gloucester Meteor and the German ME 262 never met in battle.

151
00:11:28,401 --> 00:11:33,400
The war was over before either of them could have any impact on the outcome.

152
00:11:33,401 --> 00:11:41,400
The US, lagging far behind, would not have an operational jet fighter until after the war was over.

153
00:11:41,401 --> 00:11:47,400
During the war, US military authorities initially had no idea how far behind they were.

154
00:11:47,401 --> 00:11:54,580
Aircraft engine manufacturers in the United States had shown little interest in developing

155
00:11:54,581 --> 00:11:55,580
jet engines.

156
00:11:55,581 --> 00:12:00,220
They were making millions selling piston engines to the military.

157
00:12:00,221 --> 00:12:06,520
They also had very little incentive to take great bold leaps, if you will, because of

158
00:12:06,521 --> 00:12:09,640
the funding that was provided by the military.

159
00:12:09,641 --> 00:12:13,440
The military didn't provide funding for experimental research.

160
00:12:13,441 --> 00:12:20,740
If they wanted to do research, they more or less had to get that money from amortizing the profits on production programs.

161
00:12:21,480 --> 00:12:23,203
General Hap Arnold of the U.S.

162
00:12:23,204 --> 00:12:30,040
Army Air Corps arranged for the importation of Frank Whittle's jet engine technology from Britain to the United States.

163
00:12:30,900 --> 00:12:40,378
The General Electric Company in Lynn, Massachusetts, under conditions of great secrecy rivaling the Manhattan Project, began developing an American version of the

164
00:12:40,379 --> 00:12:41,080
Whittle jet.

165
00:12:42,060 --> 00:12:47,840
The GE engine, based in an American testbed airplane called the P-59 Era Comet,

166
00:12:48,500 --> 00:12:50,940
powered the very first American jet airplane.

167
00:12:54,580 --> 00:13:00,220
Secret testing of the P-59 began in 1942 at Muroc Dry Lake, California,

168
00:13:00,740 --> 00:13:02,560
now known as Edwards Air Force Base,

169
00:13:02,960 --> 00:13:07,360
which was used as a training base for young pilots learning to fly piston engine fighters.

170
00:13:07,361 --> 00:13:14,360
To disguise the P-59, a fake wooden propeller was placed on its nose when it was moved across the base.

171
00:13:14,361 --> 00:13:19,360
Pilots on the south end of the lake were not really quite certain what was going on,

172
00:13:19,361 --> 00:13:24,360
but every now and then some would return to base with stories of an absolutely incredible encounter.

173
00:13:24,361 --> 00:13:30,360
They would be flying along and all of a sudden an airplane would fly alongside with no propeller on it whatsoever.

174
00:13:30,361 --> 00:13:35,360
They were probably even a little more disturbed when they peered up into the cockpit

175
00:13:35,361 --> 00:13:42,560
cockpit and saw what appeared to be a gorilla wearing a derby hat and waving a stogie at them.

176
00:13:43,200 --> 00:13:47,360
Well that gorilla wearing the derby and smoking the stogie was Bell's chief test pilot Jack

177
00:13:47,361 --> 00:13:48,061
Willems.

178
00:13:48,062 --> 00:13:52,800
Jack decided that it would be a thrill enough to fly a jet alongside them but he'd go

179
00:13:52,801 --> 00:13:53,501
one better.

180
00:13:53,502 --> 00:13:58,160
Went down to Hollywood, got a gorilla suit, got the stogie, got the derby and he was

181
00:13:58,161 --> 00:13:59,333
off and running.

182
00:13:59,334 --> 00:14:05,200
And apparently the shrinks here on base managed to convince these guys that they

183
00:14:05,201 --> 00:14:11,280
had not seen what in fact they had seen because after all everybody knows an airplane can't

184
00:14:11,281 --> 00:14:18,480
fly without a propeller jack woolens would later lose his life in 1946 in the crash of a plane he

185
00:14:18,481 --> 00:14:26,720
was preparing for an air race but the p-59 that woolens helped test would pave the way for an

186
00:14:26,721 --> 00:14:29,584
exciting new generation of u.s.

187
00:14:29,585 --> 00:14:35,680
aircraft the boeing 747 has carried enough passengers to equal one

188
00:14:35,681 --> 00:14:42,720
fourth of the world's population about one and a half billion people jet engines will return

189
00:14:42,721 --> 00:14:50,880
on modern marvels we now return to jet engines on modern marvels

190
00:14:50,881 --> 00:14:58,600
As testing of the very first U.S. jet continued at Muroc Dry Lake in California,

191
00:14:59,140 --> 00:15:03,680
it became evident that it was too slow to be the airplane the military was hoping for.

192
00:15:04,360 --> 00:15:08,280
The P-59 would have been a very comfortable, nice, propeller-driven fighter.

193
00:15:08,580 --> 00:15:12,140
What it is is it's basically a piston-engine propeller fighter

194
00:15:12,141 --> 00:15:14,680
without the piston engine, without the propeller,

195
00:15:14,900 --> 00:15:16,700
and with two jet engines buried in the wing roots.

196
00:15:16,701 --> 00:15:20,380
It had very little combat potential, but it was a tremendous learning tool.

197
00:15:20,880 --> 00:15:27,688
Completely unaware of the top secret P-59 program, Kelly Johnson of Lockheed went to the U.S.

198
00:15:27,689 --> 00:15:32,080
military with a plan for a radically different jet aircraft.

199
00:15:33,760 --> 00:15:38,560
Willis Hawkins was a young airplane designer working for Kelly Johnson at the time.

200
00:15:38,960 --> 00:15:47,960
Finally, a senior colonel asked him to come into his office and he said, first thing, you've got to shut up about that airplane of yours.

201
00:15:48,840 --> 00:15:56,140
Secondly, we've got a twin-engined airplane that's flying today, jet engine, and it's no damn good.

202
00:15:57,040 --> 00:16:04,480
He said, if we give you a specification for an engine that's already running, will you go home and make us a proposal?

203
00:16:05,320 --> 00:16:09,040
Two weeks later, Kelly Johnson submitted his unique proposal.

204
00:16:09,041 --> 00:16:16,080
And three weeks later, he had a contract to build America's first operational jet fighter, the P-80.

205
00:16:16,081 --> 00:16:19,840
It was the beginning of Lockheed's top secret skunkworks division,

206
00:16:20,220 --> 00:16:24,580
the advanced aircraft unit that would go on to build many legendary airplanes,

207
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:27,680
including most recently the stealth fighter.

208
00:16:29,020 --> 00:16:34,560
He was given a contract to conduct at Lockheed,

209
00:16:35,220 --> 00:16:39,700
virtually an experiment in not only in the aircraft but in production.

210
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:45,180
He demanded and received authority to have a sequestered workplace,

211
00:16:45,181 --> 00:16:49,580
to have no government interference, to be able to do a minimum of paperwork.

212
00:16:49,760 --> 00:16:54,500
Starting with the P-80 program, one of Kelly Johnson's rules at the Skunk Works

213
00:16:54,501 --> 00:16:59,420
was never to separate the engineering department from the manufacturing department.

214
00:17:00,600 --> 00:17:06,740
Willis Hawkins saw the value of that rule when he was in the office of one of the F-80's designers, Irv Culver.

215
00:17:07,440 --> 00:17:12,000
The manufacturing department on the floor below called up to say they needed a part designed

216
00:17:12,001 --> 00:17:15,220
to brace a small curved section of the bulkhead.

217
00:17:15,840 --> 00:17:17,400
Well, Irv said, let's go down to the shop.

218
00:17:17,640 --> 00:17:20,380
And he took a piece of cardboard and fiddled with it

219
00:17:20,381 --> 00:17:23,420
and cut it with scissors until it fit, bent up the edges,

220
00:17:23,940 --> 00:17:26,380
and he said, make it like that out of 060 aluminum.

221
00:17:27,160 --> 00:17:28,020
And the guy said, fine.

222
00:17:28,520 --> 00:17:31,000
And Culver said, bring it back.

223
00:17:31,040 --> 00:17:32,300
I've got to make a drawing of it.

224
00:17:32,301 --> 00:17:37,500
And that was what was meant by the communication with the shop.

225
00:17:37,501 --> 00:17:46,660
The all-new P-80 could fly at 560 miles an hour, about 150 miles an hour faster than the P-59.

226
00:17:47,800 --> 00:17:53,520
Bob Hoover, who saw his first jet from a German prison camp, was now a test pilot in the P-80 program,

227
00:17:54,160 --> 00:17:58,980
contending with early jet engines that after five hours would overheat and malfunction.

228
00:18:00,420 --> 00:18:06,580
Those days we didn't have ejection seats, and you knew you had to get it on the ground quickly,

229
00:18:06,581 --> 00:18:08,600
or you were in a lot of trouble.

230
00:18:09,120 --> 00:18:11,620
Because if the front light came on,

231
00:18:12,080 --> 00:18:14,340
that meant it's going to blow up pretty quick.

232
00:18:14,980 --> 00:18:17,200
In the world of fighter aircraft,

233
00:18:17,620 --> 00:18:20,760
models like the P-80 were designated P for pursuit.

234
00:18:21,340 --> 00:18:27,500
That changed in 1948 when the P became an F for fighter as in F-80.

235
00:18:28,380 --> 00:18:34,140
The F-80 shooting star became America's first production jet fighter in 1944.

236
00:18:34,141 --> 00:18:39,760
It led to the more advanced and powerful F-84 Thunderjet in 1946.

237
00:18:40,520 --> 00:18:41,789
It was the first U.S.

238
00:18:41,790 --> 00:18:46,080
jet to have an ejection seat, an idea that was copied from the Germans.

239
00:18:47,940 --> 00:18:52,780
Bob Hoover was the first to use one in an emergency, or at least try to use one.

240
00:18:53,680 --> 00:18:57,140
It happened when the jet engine on his test plane suddenly malfunctioned.

241
00:18:58,760 --> 00:19:01,320
It was very abrupt when one of the jet engines freezes.

242
00:19:01,321 --> 00:19:04,100
that whole airplane lurches like that.

243
00:19:04,980 --> 00:19:08,000
And now it's just pitching over, and I'm completely out of control.

244
00:19:08,860 --> 00:19:11,440
And so I thought, well, here we go with this seat.

245
00:19:12,220 --> 00:19:17,080
And so I reached down and pulled the handle, and nothing happened.

246
00:19:17,380 --> 00:19:20,600
Then I opened the canopy, and we're sucked right out.

247
00:19:21,040 --> 00:19:24,520
And the reason for the ejection seat is to get you over the tail

248
00:19:24,521 --> 00:19:27,880
to avoid killing the pilot by hitting the tail.

249
00:19:27,881 --> 00:19:35,620
I went right into the tail and broke both legs right through the knees and landed way up in the Antelope Valley there.

250
00:19:36,300 --> 00:19:45,760
Near death from shock and exposure, Hoover was eventually picked up by a farmer who saw his parachute in the sky and searched for the downed pilot for hours.

251
00:19:47,520 --> 00:19:51,700
The move into a jet fighter was a very pleasant experience.

252
00:19:51,701 --> 00:19:59,560
Marty Knutson logged more than 1,000 hours as an F-80 pilot in Korea after first flying a piston engine fighter.

253
00:20:00,220 --> 00:20:07,700
Suddenly you didn't have to worry about having the right RPM for the power you were pulling and the mixtures.

254
00:20:08,120 --> 00:20:10,500
There was none of those controls. You had to go handle.

255
00:20:11,320 --> 00:20:13,520
You had to get off or fast.

256
00:20:14,080 --> 00:20:18,540
But American jet pilots soon faced a formidable opponent in Korea.

257
00:20:18,541 --> 00:20:25,340
In November of 1950, an airplane appeared which absolutely shocked us.

258
00:20:25,420 --> 00:20:30,840
The MiG-15 is a Russian-built airplane powered by a derivative of the Rolls-Royce engine,

259
00:20:30,960 --> 00:20:35,260
which England, either through stupidity or somebody's treasonous effort,

260
00:20:35,360 --> 00:20:39,600
had sold to the Soviet Union at a time when it was very, very foolish to have done so.

261
00:20:39,900 --> 00:20:44,720
It gave the Soviet Union engine industry a massive injection of technology.

262
00:20:44,721 --> 00:20:48,600
Immediately the whole stakes in the air superiority battle changed.

263
00:20:49,940 --> 00:20:54,400
The F-80 was not really suitable to duke it out with the MiG-15.

264
00:20:54,680 --> 00:20:59,260
It simply had too many performance problems relative to the higher performance MiG.

265
00:20:59,560 --> 00:21:04,800
The North American F-86 Sabrejet, which arrived later in the Korean conflict,

266
00:21:05,280 --> 00:21:08,640
came close to matching the Soviet MiG-15 in performance.

267
00:21:08,641 --> 00:21:14,880
aircraft designers were playing catch-up with the dramatically higher speed capabilities of jet

268
00:21:14,881 --> 00:21:17,418
engines the f-86 was the first u.s.

269
00:21:17,418 --> 00:21:21,840
jet to have its wings swept back at an angle like the MiG-15s

270
00:21:21,841 --> 00:21:29,220
which created better handling at high speeds the f-86 wasn't quite as fast as the MiG-15 but flown

271
00:21:29,221 --> 00:21:35,460
by the better trained US pilots the f-86s were just enough to redress the balance in Korea

272
00:21:35,461 --> 00:21:43,460
In the early days, jet development and rocket development were closely related, and both

273
00:21:43,461 --> 00:21:46,740
were used to power experimental airplanes.

274
00:21:46,741 --> 00:21:51,120
The main difference between the two is that the jet engine uses oxygen in the air for

275
00:21:51,121 --> 00:21:56,900
its combustion, while the rocket carries oxygen on board for its combustion.

276
00:21:56,901 --> 00:22:03,740
That makes the rocket engine heavier, but allows it to operate outside the Earth's atmosphere.

277
00:22:03,741 --> 00:22:09,666
made very little distinction between them. Both were referred to as jet propulsion.

278
00:22:09,667 --> 00:22:10,367
The

279
00:22:10,368 --> 00:22:16,440
jet propulsion laboratory, for example, when it was set up, was a laboratory to investigate

280
00:22:16,441 --> 00:22:21,020
largely rockets. There was this feeding process back and forth between the two.

281
00:22:23,140 --> 00:22:30,580
In October of 1947 at Edwards Air Force Base, Chuck Yeager and the rocket-powered X-1 did

282
00:22:30,581 --> 00:22:33,481
something many had predicted would never be done.

283
00:22:33,482 --> 00:22:36,500
He flew an airplane faster than the speed of sound.

284
00:22:37,700 --> 00:22:41,940
His chase pilot on the historic flight was his good friend Bob Hoover.

285
00:22:42,500 --> 00:22:46,225
Oh, it was considered absolutely impossible.

286
00:22:46,226 --> 00:22:51,220
There was a wall of resistance that no one could penetrate.

287
00:22:51,221 --> 00:23:02,260
with Jaeger at the controls flying at nearly 700 miles an hour at 23,000 feet the rocket

288
00:23:02,261 --> 00:23:09,580
propelled Bell X-1 became the world's first supersonic airplane but jet engines soon proved

289
00:23:09,581 --> 00:23:16,480
they were just as capable as rockets of breaking the sound barrier in 1953 the North American YF-100

290
00:23:16,481 --> 00:23:20,940
became the first jet fighter to break the sound barrier in level flight.

291
00:23:21,520 --> 00:23:26,320
It was powered by the highly successful and versatile J-57 jet engine

292
00:23:26,321 --> 00:23:27,700
made by Pratt & Whitney,

293
00:23:28,180 --> 00:23:31,920
which had become the second U.S. manufacturer of jet engines

294
00:23:31,921 --> 00:23:33,620
along with General Electric.

295
00:23:39,600 --> 00:23:44,160
The Cold War of the 1950s brought a mood of fear and suspicion,

296
00:23:44,161 --> 00:23:50,280
and the jet engine found a new application in a super-secret spy plane called the U-2.

297
00:23:51,640 --> 00:23:56,160
It all started when the CIA came to Kelly Johnson at the Skunk Works with an idea.

298
00:23:57,220 --> 00:24:00,000
All the CIA said to Kelly was,

299
00:24:00,140 --> 00:24:05,380
we want something that goes a long way and gets very high,

300
00:24:05,980 --> 00:24:07,940
and we want pictures of you-know-who.

301
00:24:07,941 --> 00:24:17,380
Marty Knudsen was one of the first six Air Force pilots recruited by the CIA to fly the mystery aircraft.

302
00:24:18,100 --> 00:24:22,820
We were asked if we'd be interested in flying on a very dangerous mission.

303
00:24:23,040 --> 00:24:27,980
That would be of great value to the United States of America.

304
00:24:29,500 --> 00:24:32,060
Any red-blooded young man says yes to that.

305
00:24:32,061 --> 00:24:38,160
Some of the pilots speculated their secret mission might be to fly the first spacecraft.

306
00:24:39,100 --> 00:24:42,780
Marty Knudsen hoped it might be a new Mach 2 supersonic fighter.

307
00:24:43,920 --> 00:24:48,920
The six young fighter pilots were shocked when they were taken to a remote CIA airfield

308
00:24:48,921 --> 00:24:52,460
and shown the ugly duckling they were going to be flying.

309
00:24:53,520 --> 00:24:58,020
It didn't really look like my cup of tea, being a young hot fighter pilot,

310
00:24:58,200 --> 00:25:01,500
seeing this big, long, dangly winged thing sitting there.

311
00:25:01,501 --> 00:25:05,340
It may not have been pretty, but it was a jet.

312
00:25:06,140 --> 00:25:09,620
A specially modified engine would propel it to incredible heights.

313
00:25:10,680 --> 00:25:13,940
We immediately started flying the airplane the next day

314
00:25:13,941 --> 00:25:19,140
and being told to start laying down operational plans for missions over Russia.

315
00:25:20,060 --> 00:25:25,220
Flying the U-2 was unlike anything Marty Knudsen had ever experienced as a pilot.

316
00:25:25,720 --> 00:25:27,680
You're almost like you're living in another world.

317
00:25:27,960 --> 00:25:30,460
You can just start to see the curvature of the Earth.

318
00:25:30,461 --> 00:25:37,680
Well, I guess I'd just say it's a big ego trip to sit up there at 70,000 feet and look down at that man's puny effort and the world below you.

319
00:25:38,300 --> 00:25:42,980
The U-2 was also powered by Pratt & Whitney's popular J57 engine.

320
00:25:43,580 --> 00:25:54,140
But to allow operation at 70,000 feet and higher, the engine had to be virtually hand-built with much closer tolerances to minimize loss of air pressure at high

321
00:25:54,141 --> 00:25:54,841
altitudes.

322
00:25:55,600 --> 00:25:59,140
It was believed the U-2 could fly with impunity over the Soviet Union

323
00:25:59,141 --> 00:26:03,260
because no Soviet fighters or missiles could go high enough to shoot it down.

324
00:26:03,640 --> 00:26:05,320
And at first, that was true.

325
00:26:06,580 --> 00:26:11,560
The apparent invincibility of the U-2 lasted until May 1, 1960,

326
00:26:12,340 --> 00:26:18,080
the day U-2 pilot Francis Gary Powers took off on a fateful mission over the Soviet Union.

327
00:26:18,520 --> 00:26:23,140
I was at Buda in our way where he was supposed to land,

328
00:26:23,141 --> 00:26:26,700
and I was supposed to take the airplane off on its next flight out of there.

329
00:26:26,880 --> 00:26:29,080
I started pre-breathing oxygen to fly,

330
00:26:29,640 --> 00:26:31,800
which you need to do two hours before takeoff,

331
00:26:31,960 --> 00:26:34,120
and of course he never arrived.

332
00:26:34,680 --> 00:26:37,680
Powers had been shot down by a Russian missile and captured.

333
00:26:38,520 --> 00:26:41,180
He was sentenced to 10 years in a labor camp,

334
00:26:41,280 --> 00:26:44,040
but was released two years later in a spy exchange.

335
00:26:44,940 --> 00:26:48,160
The U.S. announced an end to the Soviet overflights,

336
00:26:48,580 --> 00:26:50,780
but the U-2 continued to fly.

337
00:26:50,781 --> 00:26:54,040
exactly where is still classified.

338
00:26:55,040 --> 00:26:57,840
Perhaps the most amazing thing about the U-2

339
00:26:57,841 --> 00:26:59,620
is that it is still flying

340
00:26:59,621 --> 00:27:02,240
on reconnaissance missions for the Air Force

341
00:27:02,241 --> 00:27:04,120
and research missions for NASA.

342
00:27:04,860 --> 00:27:06,900
We started to see a shift in aircraft development.

343
00:27:07,660 --> 00:27:10,640
Aircraft which had been designed for months of service

344
00:27:10,641 --> 00:27:11,840
or several years of service,

345
00:27:11,841 --> 00:27:13,940
now we started to see the potentiality

346
00:27:13,941 --> 00:27:17,480
of aircraft serving for decades of service.

347
00:27:17,481 --> 00:27:22,320
The U-2 isn't the only legendary Cold War jet still flying.

348
00:27:23,060 --> 00:27:26,800
The B-52 bomber has been in service since 1955,

349
00:27:27,340 --> 00:27:31,960
and like the U-2, was invaluable in the Persian Gulf in the 1990s.

350
00:27:32,540 --> 00:27:36,380
The B-52 was yet another landmark U.S. aircraft

351
00:27:36,381 --> 00:27:39,320
powered by Pratt & Whitney's J-57 engine,

352
00:27:39,780 --> 00:27:43,560
the engine that won the coveted Collier Trophy for aviation design.

353
00:27:47,480 --> 00:27:57,560
The B-52 had eight J-57 engines hanging in double pods below its huge drooping wings.

354
00:27:57,780 --> 00:28:03,120
They enabled the world's largest jet bomber to cruise at more than 600 miles per hour

355
00:28:03,121 --> 00:28:08,320
and drop its bombs from eight miles up beyond the reach of anti-aircraft fire.

356
00:28:09,200 --> 00:28:13,360
Walter Boyne flew B-52s as an Air Force pilot in Vietnam.

357
00:28:13,361 --> 00:28:19,360
When you have complete air superiority, you could send over a dump truck and drop bombs from it.

358
00:28:19,361 --> 00:28:21,360
And essentially that's what the B-52 does.

359
00:28:21,361 --> 00:28:26,360
But no one of it would ever anticipated that the airplane would have been out flying in 1999,

360
00:28:26,361 --> 00:28:30,360
or as it probably will be in 2019.

361
00:28:30,361 --> 00:28:33,360
In the Gulf War, the B-52 had two roles.

362
00:28:33,361 --> 00:28:36,360
In the longest bomber flights ever undertaken,

363
00:28:36,361 --> 00:28:41,360
the huge planes would fly nonstop for 35 hours from U.S. air bases

364
00:28:41,361 --> 00:28:46,480
air bases to release their cruise missiles and to drop conventional bombs on ground troops

365
00:28:46,481 --> 00:28:51,016
as they had done in Vietnam. B-52 attacks were devastating.

366
00:28:51,017 --> 00:28:53,400
A single plane able to carry 80

367
00:28:53,401 --> 00:28:57,862
bombs weighing a total of 75,000 pounds.

368
00:28:57,863 --> 00:29:04,220
The B-52 will fly well into the 21st century and possibly

369
00:29:04,221 --> 00:29:10,180
even in re-engined variations where we replace the eight jet engines on the airplane perhaps with

370
00:29:10,181 --> 00:29:13,020
before as the capabilities of the jet engine change,

371
00:29:13,380 --> 00:29:16,540
we realize now that airplanes in many ways are like vessels.

372
00:29:16,680 --> 00:29:18,640
We can re-engine them, we can repackage them,

373
00:29:18,940 --> 00:29:20,460
we can, if you will, reinvent them.

374
00:29:21,460 --> 00:29:27,160
Of more than 1,400 Messerschmitt Me 262s built during World War II,

375
00:29:27,580 --> 00:29:29,800
only six are known to exist today.

376
00:29:30,580 --> 00:29:34,140
Jet Engines will return on Modern Marvels.

377
00:29:34,141 --> 00:29:39,140
We now return to Jet Engines on Modern Marvels.

378
00:29:40,180 --> 00:29:41,708
Venice, California.

379
00:29:41,709 --> 00:29:47,980
In the early 1950s, it was known as a breeding ground for the beat generation,

380
00:29:47,981 --> 00:29:53,540
a gathering place for folk singers, jazz musicians, poets, and contrary thinkers.

381
00:29:54,460 --> 00:30:00,257
Roy Marquardt, a Venice resident, wasn't a beatnik, but he was a contrary thinker.

382
00:30:00,258 --> 00:30:01,600
While everyone else

383
00:30:01,601 --> 00:30:06,700
in aviation was getting excited about the new turbojet engines, Marquardt had a passionate

384
00:30:06,701 --> 00:30:10,940
interest in a radically different, much simpler jet engine, the Ramjet.

385
00:30:10,941 --> 00:30:16,120
We were always told that Roy's first Ramjet was made on a curb down in Venice,

386
00:30:16,121 --> 00:30:20,320
California, taking a hammer and some sheet metal and making a cylinder in

387
00:30:20,321 --> 00:30:25,300
which he could burn this stuff, the air and the kerosene or whatever.

388
00:30:25,301 --> 00:30:31,480
Sometimes called the flying stovepipe, the Ramjet is a metal tube with no moving

389
00:30:31,481 --> 00:30:32,181
parts.

390
00:30:32,182 --> 00:30:38,600
It works only at extremely high air speeds, high enough for the speed alone to compress air in

391
00:30:38,601 --> 00:30:43,554
the front part of the engine, where it's combined with fuel and ignited.

392
00:30:43,555 --> 00:30:45,480
The air is brought in to the

393
00:30:45,481 --> 00:30:46,984
inlet of the engine.

394
00:30:46,985 --> 00:30:53,000
In this area here, we have the fuel control system, which controls the operation

395
00:30:53,001 --> 00:30:54,318
of the Ramjet itself.

396
00:30:54,319 --> 00:30:58,900
Instead of a compressor in the front like the turbojet, the Ramjet's high

397
00:30:58,901 --> 00:31:01,780
speed alone creates the air compression required.

398
00:31:04,680 --> 00:31:12,180
The Ramjet was first used in an operational jet aircraft in 1962 when the CIA began flying

399
00:31:12,181 --> 00:31:20,700
a revolutionary new spy plane made by the Lockheed Skunk Works, the huge, graceful SR-71 Blackbird.

400
00:31:21,700 --> 00:31:27,320
Because of its stunning appearance and astounding capabilities, many still regard it today as

401
00:31:27,321 --> 00:31:28,500
the ultimate jet.

402
00:31:28,900 --> 00:31:37,540
These huge engines used a combination of turbojet and ramjet propulsion

403
00:31:37,541 --> 00:31:42,160
to power the SR-71 to three times the speed of sound.

404
00:31:43,040 --> 00:31:45,840
Mythical, almost magical to those who knew it,

405
00:31:46,240 --> 00:31:47,880
and especially those who flew it,

406
00:31:47,881 --> 00:31:53,340
the now retired Blackbird flew faster and higher than any U.S. jet has to this day.

407
00:31:53,980 --> 00:31:55,580
At least that's the official story,

408
00:31:55,581 --> 00:32:01,640
although some speculate there may be even faster spy planes today that we aren't being told about.

409
00:32:03,180 --> 00:32:09,020
The Blackbird began when the CIA brought a major design challenge to Kelly Johnson Skunk Works.

410
00:32:09,640 --> 00:32:13,800
They wanted a replacement for the U-2 which flew extremely high but was slow

411
00:32:13,801 --> 00:32:16,120
and therefore too easy to shoot down.

412
00:32:16,860 --> 00:32:20,100
The Skunk Works came up with a plane that could fly much faster

413
00:32:20,101 --> 00:32:22,520
at three times the speed of sound,

414
00:32:22,780 --> 00:32:27,300
and even higher at altitudes approaching 100,000 feet.

415
00:32:27,720 --> 00:32:30,760
One of the strongest deterrents that perhaps we had

416
00:32:30,761 --> 00:32:34,340
for aggressors all through the Cold War

417
00:32:34,341 --> 00:32:39,080
was that notion of the would-be despot, or actual despot,

418
00:32:39,860 --> 00:32:43,340
sitting down, enjoying his cup of coffee or whatever,

419
00:32:43,780 --> 00:32:47,560
and then hearing that boom and realizing that he had just been overflung.

420
00:32:50,100 --> 00:33:03,280
In the early 1950s, it was a commonly held view that while jet propulsion might make sense for military planes, the jet was impractical for use on passenger aircraft.

421
00:33:03,560 --> 00:33:09,900
For one thing, the jet was seen as a fuel guzzler and therefore not economically feasible for commercial travel.

422
00:33:10,400 --> 00:33:19,000
But that overlooked the fact that the fuel it guzzled was cheap kerosene, not the highly refined expensive aviation fuel that piston aircraft were using.

423
00:33:19,001 --> 00:33:23,000
And there was another economy the airlines were unaware of.

424
00:33:23,001 --> 00:33:28,000
Because the jet engine had virtually no vibration compared to a piston engine,

425
00:33:28,001 --> 00:33:32,000
there was much less wear and tear on the engine and the aircraft,

426
00:33:32,001 --> 00:33:37,000
and the time between overhauls, or TBO, could be increased dramatically.

427
00:33:38,000 --> 00:33:44,000
A lot of people think that the success of the jet age was because of the speed.

428
00:33:44,001 --> 00:33:46,000
The main factor was the economy.

429
00:33:46,001 --> 00:33:49,580
They would take the engines out and find they were just like new.

430
00:33:50,280 --> 00:33:54,440
And they'd put them back in again, and then they'd come back again, and they were still like new.

431
00:33:54,960 --> 00:34:01,100
The time between overhauls has soared from a few hundred hours on the piston airliners of the early 1950s

432
00:34:01,101 --> 00:34:04,780
to more than 20,000 hours on jet airliners today.

433
00:34:05,920 --> 00:34:11,160
In 1952, Britain became the first country to put a commercial passenger jet into service.

434
00:34:11,161 --> 00:34:16,620
The de Havilland Comet was celebrated as a huge breakthrough in international travel.

435
00:34:17,420 --> 00:34:19,100
But success turned to tragedy.

436
00:34:19,640 --> 00:34:22,840
After two disastrous crashes, all comets were grounded.

437
00:34:23,600 --> 00:34:27,200
The problem turned out to be cracks in the fuselage due to metal fatigue,

438
00:34:27,540 --> 00:34:30,640
which caused the pressurized cabin to eventually explode.

439
00:34:31,980 --> 00:34:38,000
The first passenger jet in the United States, the Boeing 707, went into service in 1958.

440
00:34:38,001 --> 00:34:47,160
Its design was influenced by Boeing's B-52 bomber, and it used the same engines, suspended from the wing and pods as on the B-52.

441
00:34:48,160 --> 00:34:52,740
707 was a huge improvement over any passenger plane that had ever flown.

442
00:34:54,140 --> 00:35:04,000
You can say that the jet age really began when Pan American put the 707 into service, because that was the sustained service.

443
00:35:04,260 --> 00:35:06,000
The world never looked back after that.

444
00:35:08,000 --> 00:35:16,400
From the 1950s onwards, commercial aviation stopped being the province of an elite.

445
00:35:17,160 --> 00:35:23,220
It stopped being the stuff of rolling as people did, red carpets out to an airplane as passengers boarded.

446
00:35:23,221 --> 00:35:28,320
It now became a mass means of communication and travel.

447
00:35:29,180 --> 00:35:36,260
In 1958, more people flew across the North Atlantic than sailed across it that year.

448
00:35:36,261 --> 00:35:42,580
Within 10 years, world passenger air travel had quadrupled.

449
00:35:42,581 --> 00:35:45,940
There was a famous picture on the cover of Life magazine,

450
00:35:46,480 --> 00:35:50,440
and I think it showed 42 airplanes lined up waiting to take off at Kennedy.

451
00:35:51,100 --> 00:35:53,580
And it was becoming evident that the airlines,

452
00:35:54,620 --> 00:35:57,860
and especially the busy hub airports, were becoming congested.

453
00:35:58,280 --> 00:36:00,840
And the answer was a bigger airplane.

454
00:36:00,841 --> 00:36:12,060
In 1970, Boeing, working in cooperation with Pan American Airways, produced what is still the biggest passenger jet ever, the gigantic 747.

455
00:36:12,800 --> 00:36:15,960
It was more than twice the size of a Boeing 707.

456
00:36:17,380 --> 00:36:23,960
The 747's four jet engines are so huge, you can stand inside the intakes.

457
00:36:25,960 --> 00:36:30,760
Today's 747 is very different from the one that came out in 1970.

458
00:36:30,840 --> 00:36:37,480
The engines make half as much noise, use 17% less fuel, and are more powerful.

459
00:36:38,300 --> 00:36:43,200
The original 1970 engines had about 20,000 horsepower at takeoff,

460
00:36:43,520 --> 00:36:46,960
and today's engines have about 5,000 more.

461
00:36:47,680 --> 00:36:55,160
It can carry more than 60,000 gallons of fuel and has a range of more than 8,000 nautical miles.

462
00:36:55,500 --> 00:36:59,860
That means its gas mileage is about 750 feet per gallon.

463
00:36:59,861 --> 00:37:04,360
The first 747s in the early 70s sold for $21 million.

464
00:37:05,000 --> 00:37:09,180
Today, a 747 will cost you $170 million.

465
00:37:10,160 --> 00:37:16,100
And you can order your choice of engines from either General Electric, Pratt & Whitney, or Rolls-Royce.

466
00:37:17,360 --> 00:37:21,540
It's been the flagship of the world's airlines now for almost 30 years.

467
00:37:22,700 --> 00:37:24,960
So, it's time for another one.

468
00:37:24,961 --> 00:37:31,080
The next big development in air passenger travel will be just that, big.

469
00:37:31,840 --> 00:37:35,260
Airbus is developing what some are calling the super jumbo,

470
00:37:35,261 --> 00:37:39,700
a double-deck plane half as big again as a Boeing 747

471
00:37:39,701 --> 00:37:45,220
and capable of carrying six or seven hundred people halfway around the world.

472
00:37:46,140 --> 00:37:51,840
New generation passenger jet engines being produced by GE for the Boeing 777

473
00:37:51,841 --> 00:37:54,060
are the most powerful ever.

474
00:37:54,061 --> 00:37:58,400
reaching about 40,000 horsepower at takeoff.

475
00:37:59,960 --> 00:38:04,100
Nearly 30 years after Chuck Yeager broke the sound barrier,

476
00:38:04,420 --> 00:38:09,360
the Concorde carried the first supersonic passengers in 1976.

477
00:38:10,000 --> 00:38:15,100
The Concorde's four engines developing about 16,000 horsepower each at takeoff

478
00:38:15,101 --> 00:38:17,460
propelled it to twice the speed of sound.

479
00:38:18,100 --> 00:38:21,320
The plane's development was paid for by the French and British governments

480
00:38:21,321 --> 00:38:24,580
which helped eliminate a competing American design.

481
00:38:25,440 --> 00:38:28,480
Eventually, the Concorde planes were given free of charge

482
00:38:28,481 --> 00:38:31,580
to the respective national airlines in Britain and France.

483
00:38:32,300 --> 00:38:34,580
It has never been a profit-making aircraft,

484
00:38:35,000 --> 00:38:40,220
partly because its loud sonic boom prohibits travel over populated areas.

485
00:38:40,720 --> 00:38:45,940
I call it a technical miracle, which it is, but it's an economic disaster.

486
00:38:46,720 --> 00:38:48,920
Because of the sonic boom problem,

487
00:38:48,921 --> 00:38:54,200
many airline experts are pessimistic about the expansion of supersonic passenger travel.

488
00:38:54,760 --> 00:39:00,180
But $10,000 is a drop in the bucket where military air superiority is concerned,

489
00:39:00,440 --> 00:39:05,140
and that's where supersonic aircraft will continue to push the envelope.

490
00:39:08,880 --> 00:39:13,620
The first jet versus jet combat occurred in November of 1950

491
00:39:13,621 --> 00:39:18,500
between an American F-80 and a Soviet MiG-15 over Korea.

492
00:39:18,920 --> 00:39:21,000
Modern marvels will return.

493
00:39:22,100 --> 00:39:27,960
In recent years, the jet's more glamorous cousin, the rocket engine, has been getting all the attention.

494
00:39:29,580 --> 00:39:32,380
But now the jet engine is hot again.

495
00:39:33,220 --> 00:39:38,600
Jets combined with rockets are on the cutting edge of NASA's newest technology to take us into space.

496
00:39:39,160 --> 00:39:46,020
NASA believes the revolutionary new scramjet could put payloads into orbit much cheaper than the rocket-powered space shuttle.

497
00:39:46,021 --> 00:39:53,020
But a jet engine needs air to work, so rockets which don't need air would take over beyond the Earth's atmosphere.

498
00:39:53,021 --> 00:40:01,020
The scramjet is a supersonic version of the simplest type of jet engine there is, the ramjet.

499
00:40:01,021 --> 00:40:06,020
The ramjet reaches a certain altitude, it becomes less efficient in the scramjets,

500
00:40:06,021 --> 00:40:12,020
the supersonic combustion ramjets become more efficient at the higher altitudes and the high Mach numbers.

501
00:40:12,021 --> 00:40:15,980
Mach numbers, talking about Mach numbers between Mach 8 and Mach 20.

502
00:40:16,160 --> 00:40:22,960
This is probably the only way in the future that we're going to come up with low-cost access to space.

503
00:40:23,840 --> 00:40:29,200
Another new cutting-edge development in jet engines is called thrust vectoring.

504
00:40:30,260 --> 00:40:37,020
It allows the jet's exhaust to be aimed instantly in any direction, creating great maneuverability.

505
00:40:37,021 --> 00:40:45,260
thrust vectoring technology was pioneered in the x-31 program at the nasa dryden research center in

506
00:40:45,261 --> 00:40:53,080
california the highly maneuverable x-31 staged an amazing demonstration at the paris air show

507
00:40:53,081 --> 00:41:02,460
in 1995 the air force's new fighter jet the f-22 raptor has thrust vectoring to a limited degree

508
00:41:03,360 --> 00:41:07,720
And beyond the F-22, the next-generation joint strike fighter

509
00:41:07,721 --> 00:41:10,880
that will be used by the Air Force, the Marines, and the Navy

510
00:41:10,881 --> 00:41:15,260
is expected to have even greater thrust vectoring capability.

511
00:41:16,040 --> 00:41:20,920
Eventually, some say, the jet fighter will be an unmanned air vehicle, or UAV,

512
00:41:20,921 --> 00:41:25,220
like those being developed at the Lockheed Skunk Works by Eric Knudsen.

513
00:41:26,240 --> 00:41:29,120
His father, former U-2 pilot Marty Knudsen,

514
00:41:29,121 --> 00:41:34,800
once flew jets over the Atlantic using a handheld sextant to navigate by the stars.

515
00:41:35,660 --> 00:41:39,020
Eric's unmanned planes do it all with a computer chip.

516
00:41:40,060 --> 00:41:44,040
Well, if I understand this right here, this is the man and more.

517
00:41:44,520 --> 00:41:46,860
Something like that. It's a pilot plus all the EVM.

518
00:41:47,860 --> 00:41:51,320
Small unmanned air vehicles like this Sikorsky model

519
00:41:51,321 --> 00:41:54,200
will someday fly military reconnaissance missions

520
00:41:54,201 --> 00:41:57,920
through urban canyons and even inside buildings.

521
00:41:57,921 --> 00:42:01,900
and some may be powered by tiny micro-turbine jet engines.

522
00:42:03,300 --> 00:42:09,960
The drawings and designs I've seen have engines about the diameter of a quarter or less.

523
00:42:10,880 --> 00:42:14,680
We're talking about engines that are extremely small, very lightweight,

524
00:42:15,420 --> 00:42:19,080
and probably a few years off in the future before they come to existence.

525
00:42:19,860 --> 00:42:21,864
The Skunk Works and other U.S.

526
00:42:21,865 --> 00:42:26,340
aircraft companies are also designing much larger unmanned vehicles

527
00:42:26,341 --> 00:42:30,060
that could someday fulfill the role of today's jet fighters.

528
00:42:32,780 --> 00:42:37,080
One of the greatest aircraft mysteries of all time is the Aurora,

529
00:42:37,640 --> 00:42:42,160
a top-secret U.S. spy plane rumored to be flying since the early 1990s

530
00:42:42,161 --> 00:42:44,300
at speeds as high as Mach 7.

531
00:42:44,900 --> 00:42:47,180
It's speculated the plane, if it exists,

532
00:42:47,320 --> 00:42:50,100
may use a highly advanced form of jet propulsion,

533
00:42:50,600 --> 00:42:52,380
unlike any we've seen so far.

534
00:42:53,080 --> 00:42:57,140
When the legendary SR-71 Blackbird was retired in 1990,

535
00:42:57,620 --> 00:43:01,560
many speculated the Air Force must have a new and even better spy plane.

536
00:43:02,260 --> 00:43:06,460
In the early 1990s, there were several sightings of a strange, loud aircraft

537
00:43:06,461 --> 00:43:10,460
that left an unusual contrail described as donuts on a rope.

538
00:43:11,360 --> 00:43:15,620
When the mysterious budget item, Aurora, was included on an Air Force report,

539
00:43:16,000 --> 00:43:18,740
apparently by accident, the name stuck.

540
00:43:19,500 --> 00:43:21,860
But does the Aurora really exist?

541
00:43:22,380 --> 00:43:25,500
I have to tell you that I don't know, but here's my personal opinion.

542
00:43:25,620 --> 00:43:30,600
I think that the Aurora existed, that it was flown, was not satisfactory and withdrawn.

543
00:43:31,220 --> 00:43:34,120
Now, that may be completely wrong, but that's my opinion.

544
00:43:34,540 --> 00:43:43,380
That view might be supported by the fact that the United States temporarily reactivated three SR-71 Blackbirds in 1995.

545
00:43:45,960 --> 00:43:52,100
Throughout the history of the jet engine, the most mind-boggling developments have taken place in secret.

546
00:43:52,380 --> 00:43:56,020
only to be revealed much later to an amazed public.

547
00:43:56,860 --> 00:44:01,440
Today, billions of dollars are spent each year for research, development, and production

548
00:44:01,441 --> 00:44:03,620
in secret military programs.

549
00:44:04,180 --> 00:44:09,880
If the money's being put to good use, we can prepare to be amazed at any moment.

